{"id": "3f8765b7-b2e8-4621-b6ee-08bebf46163e", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T07:02:08.910695", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "df739703-befd-4303-9a8d-990a54a19e8e", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "### Proposed Experimental Design Approach\n\n#### 1. Research Question & Objective\nThe core objective is to design an experimental study evaluating whether the proposed **MinED (Minimum Edit Distance)** alignment criterion outperforms the baseline **EM (Exact Matching)** alignment criterion on the **Big-Bench Hard (BBH)** benchmark. In free-form and chain-of-thought (CoT) generation with LLMs, alignment criteria map model responses to ground-truth candidate answers (or option labels).\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Experimental Framework & Setup\n1. **Benchmark Suite**:\n   - **Big-Bench Hard (BBH)**: 23 challenging algorithmic, symbolic, and reasoning tasks where prior standard prompting struggled (e.g., Boolean Expressions, Date Understanding, Tracking Shuffled Objects, Temporal Sequences, etc.).\n   - We will examine both Multiple-Choice (MC) tasks and Free-form / Direct generation tasks within BBH.\n\n2. **Alignment Criteria under Comparison**:\n   - **Baseline (EM - Exact Matching)**: The predicted string must match the target option/string exactly (typically after minimal standard text normalization such as stripping whitespace and lowercasing). If no exact match is found, the prediction is counted as incorrect (or extraction failure).\n   - **Proposed (MinED - Minimum Edit Distance)**: Evaluates the Levenshtein distance between the extracted candidate answer span (or generated completion) and each reference answer candidate. The candidate with the minimum edit distance is selected as the aligned prediction.\n   - **Controls & Additional Baselines (if applicable)**: Substring containment matching, semantic similarity (embedding-based or NLI-based), or LLM-as-a-judge matching.\n\n3. **Model Selection**:\n   - Representative leading LLMs across architectures and scales (e.g., 