{"id": "1ca3ef10-a1d8-481d-8de7-d62d627a09d4", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T07:02:14.338843", "source": "user", "parent_id": "c3e7d9dd-af19-4093-a362-7cfdeea6fdc4", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"The proposed approach is approved. \\n\\nRegarding the setup details:\\n- The objective is to evaluate whether the proposed MinED (Minimum Edit Distance) method for alignment criteria outperforms the EM (Exact Matching) method by measuring performance on the BBH benchmark.\\n- For alignment criteria, the comparison centers directly between EM (Exact Matching) and MinED (Minimum Edit Distance).\\n- The evaluation covers Big-Bench Hard (BBH), including 23 multiple-choice tasks and 4 free-form generation tasks, encompassing algorithmic reasoning, arithmetic reasoning, natural language understanding, world knowledge, and multilingual tasks.\\n- Note that expected empirical outcomes, specific internal hyperparameters, or unstated source paper details are unknown; treat unstated setup details as design assumptions with appropriate rationale.\\n\\nPlease proceed to provide the full, rigorous experiment design satisfying the deliverable submission contract (design rationale, controls, evaluation protocols, analysis plan, and feasibility). Do not fabricate or claim executed empirical results.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
{"id": "3ed938f3-282d-4775-b8f4-826c182bd4a1", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T07:03:27.709270", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "1ca3ef10-a1d8-481d-8de7-d62d627a09d4", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experimental Study Design: Evaluating MinED vs. EM Alignment Criteria on Big-Bench Hard (BBH)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary & Research Question\n\n### 1.1 Research Question\n> **For Alignment Criteria, does the proposed Minimum Edit Distance (MinED) method outperform the Exact Matching (EM) method on the Big-Bench Hard (BBH) benchmark?**\n\n### 1.2 Motivation & Theoretical Hypothesis\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) evaluated on complex reasoning tasks—such as those in Big-Bench Hard (BBH)—frequently generate extended Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning traces concluding with natural language statements. In standard evaluation pipelines, an **alignment criterion** is required to map the generated terminal output string to a canonical ground-truth answer or discrete candidate option.\n- **Exact Matching (EM)**: Enforces strict equality (or basic normalized string equality) between the extracted prediction and ground-truth targets. While precise, EM is vulnerable to false negatives caused by extraneous tokens, minor formatting variations, punctuation discrepancies, or morphological inflections.\n- **Minimum Edit Distance (MinED)**: Measures character/token-level Levenshtein edit distance between the model's generated answer span and canonical candidate answers, selecting the candidate that minimizes this distance.\n- **Hypothesis ($H_1$)**: MinED achieves statistically significantly higher task-level and macro-average accuracy than EM across BBH by resolving benign lexical and syntactic formatting misalignments without inflating false-positive rates.\n- **Null Hypothesis ($H_0$)**: There is no statistically significant performance difference between MinED and EM on BBH, or MinED introduces false-positive alignments that degrade alignment fidelity.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Benchmark Suite & Task Taxonomy\n\nThe evaluation encompasses the full Big-Bench Hard (BBH) suite, spanning **27 tasks** (comprising 23 multiple-choice tasks and 4 free-form generation tasks) across five core cognitive domains:\n\n### 2.1 Domain & Task Breakdown\n1. **Algorithmic Reasoning (Symbolic & State Tracking)**:\n   - *Tasks*: `boolean_expressions`, `dyck_languages`, `navigate`, `tracking_shuffled_objects_three_objects`, `tracking_shuffled_objects_five_objects`, `tracking_shuffled_objects_seven_objects`, `web_of_lies`, `word_sorting`.\n   - *Target Format*: Both multiple-choice choice labels/text (e.g., `(A)`, `(B)`) and free-form structured sequences (e.g., sorted words, parenthesis sequences).\n2. **Arithmetic & Quantitative Reasoning**:\n   - *Tasks*: `multistep_arithmetic_two`, `object_counting`, `penguins_in_a_table`, `reasoning_about_colored_objects`.\n   - *Target Format*: Direct integers, numeric quantities, and discrete categorical values.\n3. **Natural Language Understanding & Logic**:\n   - *Tasks*: `causal_judgement`, `date_understanding`, `disambiguation_qa`, `formal_fallacies`, `logical_deduction_three_objects`, `logical_deduction_five_objects`, `logical_deduction_seven_objects`, `snarks`, `temporal_sequences`.\n   - *Target Format*: Multi-choice selections from constrained semantic categories.\n4. **World Knowledge & Commonsense**:\n   - *Tasks*: `geometric_shapes`, `hyperbaton`, `movie_recommendation`, `ruin_names`, `salient_translation_error_detection`, `sports_understanding`.\n   - *Target Format*: Domain-specific classification and candidate entity selection.\n5. **Multilingual & Cross-Lingual Tasks**:\n   - Tasks with multilingual elements (e.g., `salient_translation_error_detection`).\n\n### 2.2 Dataset Scale\n- Each subtask contains 250 evaluation instances (totaling 6,750 evaluation instances across the 27 subtasks), drawn from the canonical Big-Bench Hard release (Suzgun et al., 2022).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Mathematical Formulation of Alignment Criteria\n\nLet an evaluation instance $i$ consist of an input prompt $x_i$, a set of canonical target candidate strings $\\mathcal{C}_i = \\{c_{i,1}, c_{i,2}, \\dots, c_{i,K}\\}$, and ground-truth target $y^*_i \\in \\mathcal{C}_i$.\nLet the LLM output completion be $\\hat{T}_i$, from which a target prediction span $\\hat{y}_i = \\text{Extract}(\\hat{T}_i)$ is isolated using a standardized extraction heuristic (e.g., regex matching on trigger phrases like `\"The answer is\"` or terminal line isolation).\nLet $\\phi(s)$ denote standard lexical normalization: lowercasing, unicode normalization (NFKC), and leading/trailing whitespace trimming.\n\n### 3.1 Baseline: Exact Matching (EM)\nUnder EM, the extracted span $\\hat{y}_i$ is aligned to candidate $c \\in \\mathcal{C}_i$ if and only if their normalized forms match identically:\n$$\\text{Align}_{\\text{EM}}(\\hat{y}_i, \\mathcal{C}_i) = \\begin{cases} c & \\text{if } \\exists !\\, c \\in \\mathcal{C}_i \\text{ s.t. } \\phi(\\hat{y}_i) = \\phi(c) \\\\ \\text{null} & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}$$\nThe binary correctness score is:\n$$S_{\\text{EM}}(i) = \\mathbb{I}\\left(\\text{Align}_{\\text{EM}}(\\hat{y}_i, \\mathcal{C}_i) = y^*_i\\right)$$\n\n### 3.2 Proposed Method: Minimum Edit Distance (MinED)\nLet $\\text{ED}(s_1, s_2)$ denote the Levenshtein distance between strings $s_1$ and $s_2$ (minimum number of single-character insertions, deletions, and substitutions).\n\n1. **Multiple-Choice Alignment (Choice-Constrained)**:\n   For tasks with candidate choices $\\mathcal{C}_i$:\n   $$\\hat{c}_i^* = \\arg\\min_{c \\in \\mathcal{C}_i} \\text{ED}\\left(\\phi(\\hat{y}_i), \\phi(c)\\right)$$\n   To prevent ungrounded alignment on wildly aberrant or hallucinated completions, an acceptance threshold $\\tau_{\\text{norm}}$ is enforced:\n   $$\\text{Align}_{\\text{MinED}}(\\hat{y}_i, \\mathcal{C}_i) = \\begin{cases} \\hat{c}_i^* & \\text{if } \\frac{\\text{ED}(\\phi(\\hat{y}_i), \\phi(\\hat{c}_i^*))}{\\max(|\\phi(\\hat{y}_i)|, |\\phi(\\hat{c}_i^*)|)} \\le \\tau_{\\text{norm}} \\\\ \\text{null} & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}$$\n   *(Default threshold: $\\tau_{\\text{norm}} = 0.40$ for text choices; strict 0-distance for single-digit arithmetic where edit distance 1 constitutes a substantive numeric error).*\n\n2. **Free-Form Generation Alignment**:\n   In free-form tasks where $\\mathcal{C}_i = \\{y^*_i\\}$ (single gold reference or set of acceptable reference paraphrases):\n   $$\\text{Align}_{\\text{MinED}}(\\hat{y}_i, y^*_i) = \\begin{cases} y^*_i & \\text{if } \\text{ED}(\\phi(\\hat{y}_i), \\phi(y^*_i)) \\le \\tau_{\\text{abs}} \\text{ or } \\frac{\\text{ED}(\\phi(\\hat{y}_i), \\phi(y^*_i))}{\\max(|\\phi(\\hat{y}_i)|, |y^*_i|)} \\le \\tau_{\\text{norm}} \\\\ \\text{null} & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}$$\n   The binary correctness score is:\n   $$S_{\\text{MinED}}(i) = \\mathbb{I}\\left(\\text{Align}_{\\text{MinED}}(\\hat{y}_i, \\mathcal{C}_i) = y^*_i\\right)$$\n\n3. **Tie-Breaking Rule**:\n   If $\\arg\\min_{c \\in \\mathcal{C}_i} \\text{ED}(\\phi(\\hat{y}_i), \\phi(c))$ yields multiple candidates with identical minimum distance, the instance is flagged as ambiguous and scored as incorrect (`null`).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Experimental Setup & Controls\n\n### 4.1 Model Suite\nTo prevent model-specific artifacts from confounding the comparison, the evaluation spans diverse LLM architectures and parameter regimes:\n1. **Open-Weights Foundational LLMs**:\n   - `LLaMA-2-7B`, `LLaMA-2-13B`, `LLaMA-2-70B`\n   - `LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct`, `LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct`\n   - `Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2`, `Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct`\n2. **Frontier API Models**:\n   - `GPT-3.5-Turbo-0125`\n   - `GPT-4o` / `GPT-4-Turbo`\n\n### 4.2 Prompting & Inference Protocol\n- **Demonstration Setup**: Standard few-shot Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompts using the canonical 3-shot demonstrations per BBH task specified by Suzgun et al. (2022).\n- **Prompt Structure**:\n  ```text\n  [Instruction & Context]\n  Q: <Demonstration Question 1>\n  A: <Step-by-step reasoning> So the answer is <Demonstration Answer 1>.\n  ...\n  Q: <Test Question>\n  A: <Model Output Reasoning Chain>\n  ```\n- **Inference Decoding Parameters**:\n  - `temperature`: $0.0$ (greedy decoding for strict reproducibility)\n  - `top_p`: $1.0$\n  - `max_new_tokens`: $512$ (sufficient for full reasoning traces across all BBH subtasks)\n  - `repetition_penalty`: $1.0$\n  - `stop_sequences`: `[\"\\n\\nQ:\", \"\\n\\n---\"]`\n\n### 4.3 Experimental Controls & Mitigations\n1. **Candidate Target Definition Control**:\n   - For multiple-choice tasks, candidates are evaluated under two representations:\n     - *Label Matching*: Candidate set is the option keys $\\{\\text{\"(A)\"}, \\text{\"(B)\"}, \\dots\\}$.\n     - *Text Matching*: Candidate set is the verbatim choice texts $\\{\\text{\"choice text A\"}, \\text{\"choice text B\"}, \\dots\\}$.\n   - If the model generates \"The answer is A: <text>\", both label-based and full-text MinED alignments are tested to prevent bias from option key extraction.\n2. **Numeric Precision Guard (Arithmetic Control)**:\n   - For integer/numeric tasks (`multistep_arithmetic_two`, `object_counting`), substitution of a digit represents a factual error (e.g., `42` vs `43` has Levenshtein distance 1, but is factually incorrect). MinED is constrained to strict numeric equivalence for pure number targets, or operates with edit distance threshold $\\tau = 0$ on numeric strings.\n3. **Extraction Heuristic Ablation**:\n   - Regex-based span isolation: evaluate whether MinED benefits occur predominantly when regex fails to isolate the final answer, or when applied to the full terminal sentence.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Evaluation Protocol & Metrics\n\n### 5.1 Primary Performance Metrics\n1. **Per-Task Accuracy**:\n   $$\\text{Acc}_k = \\frac{1}{N_k} \\sum_{i=1}^{N_k} S(i) \\quad \\text{for } k \\in \\{1, \\dots, 27\\}$$\n2. **Macro-Average BBH Accuracy**:\n   $$\\text{Acc}_{\\text{Macro}} = \\frac{1}{27} \\sum_{k=1}^{27} \\text{Acc}_k$$\n3. **Task-Group Stratified Accuracy**:\n   - Multiple-Choice Accuracy (23 tasks)\n   - Free-form Generation Accuracy (4 tasks)\n   - Cognitive Domain Accuracies (Algorithmic, Arithmetic, NLU, World Knowledge, Multilingual)\n\n### 5.2 Diagnostic & Alignment Fidelity Metrics\n1. **Extraction / Alignment Failure Rate**:\n   - Percentage of instances yielding `null` alignment under EM vs. MinED.\n2. **Alignment Divergence / Flip Analysis**:\n   - **Gain Rate ($EM \\to 0, MinED \\to 1$)**: Instances where EM fails due to superficial formatting/spelling variation, but MinED recovers the ground-truth target.\n   - **Loss Rate ($EM \\to 1, MinED \\to 0$)**: Theoretical regressions where MinED might misassign an exact match due to tie-breaks or thresholding.\n   - **False Positive Flip Rate ($EM \\to 0, MinED \\to \\text{Wrong Target}$)**: Instances where MinED forces an alignment to an incorrect choice rather than flagging an invalid answer.\n3. **Human Validation / Alignment Precision**:\n   - Blinded dual-annotator human evaluation on a stratified random sample of $N = 300$ instances where $S_{\\text{MinED}} \\neq S_{\\text{EM}}$. Annotators judge whether the LLM's generated reasoning chain genuinely concluded with the target answer.\n   - Inter-annotator agreement measured via Cohen's $\\kappa$.\n\n### 5.3 Statistical Hypothesis Testing Plan\n- **Task-Level Significance**: Two-tailed paired McNemar's test on the binary response contingency table ($n_{01}$ vs. $n_{10}$) for each of the 27 subtasks:\n  $$\\chi^2 = \\frac{(|n_{01} - n_{10}| - 1)^2}{n_{01} + n_{10}}, \\quad p < 0.05$$\n- **Multiple Testing Correction**: Benjamini-Hochberg False Discovery Rate (FDR) adjustment across the 27 hypothesis tests.\n- **Macro-Level Significance**: Non-parametric paired bootstrap resampling ($B = 10,000$ iterations) computing 95% confidence intervals on $\\Delta \\text{Acc}_{\\text{Macro}} = \\text{Acc}_{\\text{Macro}}^{\\text{MinED}} - \\text{Acc}_{\\text{Macro}}^{\\text{EM}}$.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Execution Feasibility, Risk Analysis & Threats to Validity\n\n| Potential Threat / Risk | Consequence | Mitigation Strategy in Design |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Numeric False Positives** | In arithmetic tasks, Levenshtein distance 1 (e.g., \"15\" vs \"16\") is mathematically wrong. | Set zero edit-distance tolerance ($\\tau = 0$) for numeric tokens; apply MinED strictly to non-numeric linguistic tokens. |\n| **Short Target Degeneracy** | Single letter candidates (`A`, `B`, `C`) have small distance to many single character typos. | Normalize candidate targets to bracketed form `(A)`, `(B)` and evaluate against full choice description strings. |\n| **Greedy Tie Collisions** | Model output equidistant between two distinct multiple-choice options. | Explicitly define tie handling: mark equidistant candidates as unaligned (`null`), counting as incorrect. |\n| **Compute / Time Feasibility** | 6,750 prompts $\\times$ multiple models can be slow. | Pre-generate and cache all model completions $\\hat{T}_i$ once. Run alignment scoring (EM and MinED) offline in seconds as post-processing. |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Synthesis: Decisional Framework for Answering the Question\n\nThe experiment definitively resolves the research question based on the following empirical decision criteria:\n1. **MinED Outperforms EM** if:\n   - Macro-average accuracy $\\Delta \\text{Acc}_{\\text{Macro}} > 0$ with $p < 0.01$ via paired bootstrap testing;\n   - Statistically significant positive gains are demonstrated across multiple-choice and free-form subsets without task-level degradation;\n   - Human validation confirms $\\ge 90\\%$ of positive flips ($EM=0, MinED=1$) reflect valid semantic alignments rather than false positives.\n2. **MinED Does Not Outperform EM** if:\n   - $\\Delta \\text{Acc}_{\\text{Macro}}$ is statistically indistinguishable from zero ($p \\ge 0.05$); or\n   - Gains in lexical tasks are offset by spurious alignments in algorithmic or arithmetic reasoning tasks.\n\nThis experiment design guarantees complete reproducibility, rigorous baseline comparability, and diagnostic validation to isolate the true utility of MinED as an alignment criterion.", "evidence": "Inspected workspace and confirmed system parameters (system Python, bubblewrap environment, no pre-installed empirical data or GPU compute). The design is fully specified for offline post-processing evaluation on BBH outputs.", "assumptions": ["The Big-Bench Hard benchmark spans 27 subtasks (23 multiple-choice and 4 free-form generation tasks) across algorithmic, arithmetic, NLU, world knowledge, and multilingual domains.", "All LLM completions can be generated once using standard 3-shot CoT prompting under greedy decoding (temperature 0), allowing both EM and MinED alignment criteria to be evaluated deterministically on identical outputs.", "For arithmetic and numeric tasks, single-digit differences represent substantive factual errors, requiring a zero edit-distance tolerance rule to prevent false-positive inflation."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the original source paper utilized normalized character-level Levenshtein distance or token-level edit distance.", "The exact empirical hyperparameter threshold (tau) used in the source paper for free-form versus multiple-choice tasks."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_571014__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_571014__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"uncertainties\": [\"Whether the original source paper utilized normalized character-level Levenshtein distance or token-level edit distance.\", \"The exact empirical hyperparameter threshold (tau) used in the source paper for free-form versus multiple-choice tasks.\"], \"evidence\": \"Inspected workspace and confirmed system parameters (system Python, bubblewrap environment, no pre-installed empirical data or GPU compute). The design is fully specified for offline post-processing evaluation on BBH outputs.\", \"assumptions\": [\"The Big-Bench Hard benchmark spans 27 subtasks (23 multiple-choice and 4 free-form generation tasks) across algorithmic, arithmetic, NLU, world knowledge, and multilingual domains.\", \"All LLM completions can be generated once using standard 3-shot CoT prompting under greedy decoding (temperature 0), allowing both EM and MinED alignment criteria to be evaluated deterministically on identical outputs.\", \"For arithmetic and numeric tasks, single-digit differences represent substantive factual errors, requiring a zero edit-distance tolerance rule to prevent false-positive inflation.\"], \"answer\": \"# Experimental Study Design: Evaluating MinED vs. EM Alignment Criteria on Big-Bench Hard (BBH)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Executive Summary & Research Question\\n\\n### 1.1 Research Question\\n> **For Alignment Criteria, does the proposed Minimum Edit Distance (MinED) method outperform the Exact Matching (EM) method on the Big-Bench Hard (BBH) benchmark?**\\n\\n### 1.2 Motivation & Theoretical Hypothesis\\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) evaluated on complex reasoning tasks\\u2014such as those in Big-Bench Hard (BBH)\\u2014frequently generate extended Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning traces concluding with natural language statements. In standard evaluation pipelines, an **alignment criterion** is required to map the generated terminal output string to a canonical ground-truth answer or discrete candidate option.\\n- **Exact Matching (EM)**: Enforces strict equality (or basic normalized string equality) between the extracted prediction and ground-truth targets. While precise, EM is vulnerable to false negatives caused by extraneous tokens, minor formatting variations, punctuation discrepancies, or morphological inflections.\\n- **Minimum Edit Distance (MinED)**: Measures character/token-level Levenshtein edit distance between the model's generated answer span and canonical candidate answers, selecting the candidate that minimizes this distance.\\n- **Hypothesis ($H_1$)**: MinED achieves statistically significantly higher task-level and macro-average accuracy than EM across BBH by resolving benign lexical and syntactic formatting misalignments without inflating false-positive rates.\\n- **Null Hypothesis ($H_0$)**: There is no statistically significant performance difference between MinED and EM on BBH, or MinED introduces false-positive alignments that degrade alignment fidelity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Benchmark Suite & Task Taxonomy\\n\\nThe evaluation encompasses the full Big-Bench Hard (BBH) suite, spanning **27 tasks** (comprising 23 multiple-choice tasks and 4 free-form generation tasks) across five core cognitive domains:\\n\\n### 2.1 Domain & Task Breakdown\\n1. **Algorithmic Reasoning (Symbolic & State Tracking)**:\\n   - *Tasks*: `boolean_expressions`, `dyck_languages`, `navigate`, `tracking_shuffled_objects_three_objects`, `tracking_shuffled_objects_five_objects`, `tracking_shuffled_objects_seven_objects`, `web_of_lies`, `word_sorting`.\\n   - *Target Format*: Both multiple-choice choice labels/text (e.g., `(A)`, `(B)`) and free-form structured sequences (e.g., sorted words, parenthesis sequences).\\n2. **Arithmetic & Quantitative Reasoning**:\\n   - *Tasks*: `multistep_arithmetic_two`, `object_counting`, `penguins_in_a_table`, `reasoning_about_colored_objects`.\\n   - *Target Format*: Direct integers, numeric quantities, and discrete categorical values.\\n3. **Natural Language Understanding & Logic**:\\n   - *Tasks*: `causal_judgement`, `date_understanding`, `disambiguation_qa`, `formal_fallacies`, `logical_deduction_three_objects`, `logical_deduction_five_objects`, `logical_deduction_seven_objects`, `snarks`, `temporal_sequences`.\\n   - *Target Format*: Multi-choice selections from constrained semantic categories.\\n4. **World Knowledge & Commonsense**:\\n   - *Tasks*: `geometric_shapes`, `hyperbaton`, `movie_recommendation`, `ruin_names`, `salient_translation_error_detection`, `sports_understanding`.\\n   - *Target Format*: Domain-specific classification and candidate entity selection.\\n5. **Multilingual & Cross-Lingual Tasks**:\\n   - Tasks with multilingual elements (e.g., `salient_translation_error_detection`).\\n\\n### 2.2 Dataset Scale\\n- Each subtask contains 250 evaluation instances (totaling 6,750 evaluation instances across the 27 subtasks), drawn from the canonical Big-Bench Hard release (Suzgun et al., 2022).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Mathematical Formulation of Alignment Criteria\\n\\nLet an evaluation instance $i$ consist of an input prompt $x_i$, a set of canonical target candidate strings $\\\\mathcal{C}_i = \\\\{c_{i,1}, c_{i,2}, \\\\dots, c_{i,K}\\\\}$, and ground-truth target $y^*_i \\\\in \\\\mathcal{C}_i$.\\nLet the LLM output completion be $\\\\hat{T}_i$, from which a target prediction span $\\\\hat{y}_i = \\\\text{Extract}(\\\\hat{T}_i)$ is isolated using a standardized extraction heuristic (e.g., regex matching on trigger phrases like `\\\"The answer is\\\"` or terminal line isolation).\\nLet $\\\\phi(s)$ denote standard lexical normalization: lowercasing, unicode normalization (NFKC), and leading/trailing whitespace trimming.\\n\\n### 3.1 Baseline: Exact Matching (EM)\\nUnder EM, the extracted span $\\\\hat{y}_i$ is aligned to candidate $c \\\\in \\\\mathcal{C}_i$ if and only if their normalized forms match identically:\\n$$\\\\text{Align}_{\\\\text{EM}}(\\\\hat{y}_i, \\\\mathcal{C}_i) = \\\\begin{cases} c & \\\\text{if } \\\\exists !\\\\, c \\\\in \\\\mathcal{C}_i \\\\text{ s.t. } \\\\phi(\\\\hat{y}_i) = \\\\phi(c) \\\\\\\\ \\\\text{null} & \\\\text{otherwise} \\\\end{cases}$$\\nThe binary correctness score is:\\n$$S_{\\\\text{EM}}(i) = \\\\mathbb{I}\\\\left(\\\\text{Align}_{\\\\text{EM}}(\\\\hat{y}_i, \\\\mathcal{C}_i) = y^*_i\\\\right)$$\\n\\n### 3.2 Proposed Method: Minimum Edit Distance (MinED)\\nLet $\\\\text{ED}(s_1, s_2)$ denote the Levenshtein distance between strings $s_1$ and $s_2$ (minimum number of single-character insertions, deletions, and substitutions).\\n\\n1. **Multiple-Choice Alignment (Choice-Constrained)**:\\n   For tasks with candidate choices $\\\\mathcal{C}_i$:\\n   $$\\\\hat{c}_i^* = \\\\arg\\\\min_{c \\\\in \\\\mathcal{C}_i} \\\\text{ED}\\\\left(\\\\phi(\\\\hat{y}_i), \\\\phi(c)\\\\right)$$\\n   To prevent ungrounded alignment on wildly aberrant or hallucinated completions, an acceptance threshold $\\\\tau_{\\\\text{norm}}$ is enforced:\\n   $$\\\\text{Align}_{\\\\text{MinED}}(\\\\hat{y}_i, \\\\mathcal{C}_i) = \\\\begin{cases} \\\\hat{c}_i^* & \\\\text{if } \\\\frac{\\\\text{ED}(\\\\phi(\\\\hat{y}_i), \\\\phi(\\\\hat{c}_i^*))}{\\\\max(|\\\\phi(\\\\hat{y}_i)|, |\\\\phi(\\\\hat{c}_i^*)|)} \\\\le \\\\tau_{\\\\text{norm}} \\\\\\\\ \\\\text{null} & \\\\text{otherwise} \\\\end{cases}$$\\n   *(Default threshold: $\\\\tau_{\\\\text{norm}} = 0.40$ for text choices; strict 0-distance for single-digit arithmetic where edit distance 1 constitutes a substantive numeric error).*\\n\\n2. **Free-Form Generation Alignment**:\\n   In free-form tasks where $\\\\mathcal{C}_i = \\\\{y^*_i\\\\}$ (single gold reference or set of acceptable reference paraphrases):\\n   $$\\\\text{Align}_{\\\\text{MinED}}(\\\\hat{y}_i, y^*_i) = \\\\begin{cases} y^*_i & \\\\text{if } \\\\text{ED}(\\\\phi(\\\\hat{y}_i), \\\\phi(y^*_i)) \\\\le \\\\tau_{\\\\text{abs}} \\\\text{ or } \\\\frac{\\\\text{ED}(\\\\phi(\\\\hat{y}_i), \\\\phi(y^*_i))}{\\\\max(|\\\\phi(\\\\hat{y}_i)|, |y^*_i|)} \\\\le \\\\tau_{\\\\text{norm}} \\\\\\\\ \\\\text{null} & \\\\text{otherwise} \\\\end{cases}$$\\n   The binary correctness score is:\\n   $$S_{\\\\text{MinED}}(i) = \\\\mathbb{I}\\\\left(\\\\text{Align}_{\\\\text{MinED}}(\\\\hat{y}_i, \\\\mathcal{C}_i) = y^*_i\\\\right)$$\\n\\n3. **Tie-Breaking Rule**:\\n   If $\\\\arg\\\\min_{c \\\\in \\\\mathcal{C}_i} \\\\text{ED}(\\\\phi(\\\\hat{y}_i), \\\\phi(c))$ yields multiple candidates with identical minimum distance, the instance is flagged as ambiguous and scored as incorrect (`null`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Experimental Setup & Controls\\n\\n### 4.1 Model Suite\\nTo prevent model-specific artifacts from confounding the comparison, the evaluation spans diverse LLM architectures and parameter regimes:\\n1. **Open-Weights Foundational LLMs**:\\n   - `LLaMA-2-7B`, `LLaMA-2-13B`, `LLaMA-2-70B`\\n   - `LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct`, `LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct`\\n   - `Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2`, `Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct`\\n2. **Frontier API Models**:\\n   - `GPT-3.5-Turbo-0125`\\n   - `GPT-4o` / `GPT-4-Turbo`\\n\\n### 4.2 Prompting & Inference Protocol\\n- **Demonstration Setup**: Standard few-shot Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompts using the canonical 3-shot demonstrations per BBH task specified by Suzgun et al. (2022).\\n- **Prompt Structure**:\\n  ```text\\n  [Instruction & Context]\\n  Q: <Demonstration Question 1>\\n  A: <Step-by-step reasoning> So the answer is <Demonstration Answer 1>.\\n  ...\\n  Q: <Test Question>\\n  A: <Model Output Reasoning Chain>\\n  ```\\n- **Inference Decoding Parameters**:\\n  - `temperature`: $0.0$ (greedy decoding for strict reproducibility)\\n  - `top_p`: $1.0$\\n  - `max_new_tokens`: $512$ (sufficient for full reasoning traces across all BBH subtasks)\\n  - `repetition_penalty`: $1.0$\\n  - `stop_sequences`: `[\\\"\\\\n\\\\nQ:\\\", \\\"\\\\n\\\\n---\\\"]`\\n\\n### 4.3 Experimental Controls & Mitigations\\n1. **Candidate Target Definition Control**:\\n   - For multiple-choice tasks, candidates are evaluated under two representations:\\n     - *Label Matching*: Candidate set is the option keys $\\\\{\\\\text{\\\"(A)\\\"}, \\\\text{\\\"(B)\\\"}, \\\\dots\\\\}$.\\n     - *Text Matching*: Candidate set is the verbatim choice texts $\\\\{\\\\text{\\\"choice text A\\\"}, \\\\text{\\\"choice text B\\\"}, \\\\dots\\\\}$.\\n   - If the model generates \\\"The answer is A: <text>\\\", both label-based and full-text MinED alignments are tested to prevent bias from option key extraction.\\n2. **Numeric Precision Guard (Arithmetic Control)**:\\n   - For integer/numeric tasks (`multistep_arithmetic_two`, `object_counting`), substitution of a digit represents a factual error (e.g., `42` vs `43` has Levenshtein distance 1, but is factually incorrect). MinED is constrained to strict numeric equivalence for pure number targets, or operates with edit distance threshold $\\\\tau = 0$ on numeric strings.\\n3. **Extraction Heuristic Ablation**:\\n   - Regex-based span isolation: evaluate whether MinED benefits occur predominantly when regex fails to isolate the final answer, or when applied to the full terminal sentence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Evaluation Protocol & Metrics\\n\\n### 5.1 Primary Performance Metrics\\n1. **Per-Task Accuracy**:\\n   $$\\\\text{Acc}_k = \\\\frac{1}{N_k} \\\\sum_{i=1}^{N_k} S(i) \\\\quad \\\\text{for } k \\\\in \\\\{1, \\\\dots, 27\\\\}$$\\n2. **Macro-Average BBH Accuracy**:\\n   $$\\\\text{Acc}_{\\\\text{Macro}} = \\\\frac{1}{27} \\\\sum_{k=1}^{27} \\\\text{Acc}_k$$\\n3. **Task-Group Stratified Accuracy**:\\n   - Multiple-Choice Accuracy (23 tasks)\\n   - Free-form Generation Accuracy (4 tasks)\\n   - Cognitive Domain Accuracies (Algorithmic, Arithmetic, NLU, World Knowledge, Multilingual)\\n\\n### 5.2 Diagnostic & Alignment Fidelity Metrics\\n1. **Extraction / Alignment Failure Rate**:\\n   - Percentage of instances yielding `null` alignment under EM vs. MinED.\\n2. **Alignment Divergence / Flip Analysis**:\\n   - **Gain Rate ($EM \\\\to 0, MinED \\\\to 1$)**: Instances where EM fails due to superficial formatting/spelling variation, but MinED recovers the ground-truth target.\\n   - **Loss Rate ($EM \\\\to 1, MinED \\\\to 0$)**: Theoretical regressions where MinED might misassign an exact match due to tie-breaks or thresholding.\\n   - **False Positive Flip Rate ($EM \\\\to 0, MinED \\\\to \\\\text{Wrong Target}$)**: Instances where MinED forces an alignment to an incorrect choice rather than flagging an invalid answer.\\n3. **Human Validation / Alignment Precision**:\\n   - Blinded dual-annotator human evaluation on a stratified random sample of $N = 300$ instances where $S_{\\\\text{MinED}} \\\\neq S_{\\\\text{EM}}$. Annotators judge whether the LLM's generated reasoning chain genuinely concluded with the target answer.\\n   - Inter-annotator agreement measured via Cohen's $\\\\kappa$.\\n\\n### 5.3 Statistical Hypothesis Testing Plan\\n- **Task-Level Significance**: Two-tailed paired McNemar's test on the binary response contingency table ($n_{01}$ vs. $n_{10}$) for each of the 27 subtasks:\\n  $$\\\\chi^2 = \\\\frac{(|n_{01} - n_{10}| - 1)^2}{n_{01} + n_{10}}, \\\\quad p < 0.05$$\\n- **Multiple Testing Correction**: Benjamini-Hochberg False Discovery Rate (FDR) adjustment across the 27 hypothesis tests.\\n- **Macro-Level Significance**: Non-parametric paired bootstrap resampling ($B = 10,000$ iterations) computing 95% confidence intervals on $\\\\Delta \\\\text{Acc}_{\\\\text{Macro}} = \\\\text{Acc}_{\\\\text{Macro}}^{\\\\text{MinED}} - \\\\text{Acc}_{\\\\text{Macro}}^{\\\\text{EM}}$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Execution Feasibility, Risk Analysis & Threats to Validity\\n\\n| Potential Threat / Risk | Consequence | Mitigation Strategy in Design |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Numeric False Positives** | In arithmetic tasks, Levenshtein distance 1 (e.g., \\\"15\\\" vs \\\"16\\\") is mathematically wrong. | Set zero edit-distance tolerance ($\\\\tau = 0$) for numeric tokens; apply MinED strictly to non-numeric linguistic tokens. |\\n| **Short Target Degeneracy** | Single letter candidates (`A`, `B`, `C`) have small distance to many single character typos. | Normalize candidate targets to bracketed form `(A)`, `(B)` and evaluate against full choice description strings. |\\n| **Greedy Tie Collisions** | Model output equidistant between two distinct multiple-choice options. | Explicitly define tie handling: mark equidistant candidates as unaligned (`null`), counting as incorrect. |\\n| **Compute / Time Feasibility** | 6,750 prompts $\\\\times$ multiple models can be slow. | Pre-generate and cache all model completions $\\\\hat{T}_i$ once. Run alignment scoring (EM and MinED) offline in seconds as post-processing. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Synthesis: Decisional Framework for Answering the Question\\n\\nThe experiment definitively resolves the research question based on the following empirical decision criteria:\\n1. **MinED Outperforms EM** if:\\n   - Macro-average accuracy $\\\\Delta \\\\text{Acc}_{\\\\text{Macro}} > 0$ with $p < 0.01$ via paired bootstrap testing;\\n   - Statistically significant positive gains are demonstrated across multiple-choice and free-form subsets without task-level degradation;\\n   - Human validation confirms $\\\\ge 90\\\\%$ of positive flips ($EM=0, MinED=1$) reflect valid semantic alignments rather than false positives.\\n2. **MinED Does Not Outperform EM** if:\\n   - $\\\\Delta \\\\text{Acc}_{\\\\text{Macro}}$ is statistically indistinguishable from zero ($p \\\\ge 0.05$); or\\n   - Gains in lexical tasks are offset by spurious alignments in algorithmic or arithmetic reasoning tasks.\\n\\nThis experiment design guarantees complete reproducibility, rigorous baseline comparability, and diagnostic validation to isolate the true utility of MinED as an alignment criterion.\", \"summary\": \"Complete experiment design evaluating MinED vs EM on BBH.\", \"stage\": \"result\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "ZtHUatvUKJGmq8YPicHcyAQ", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 17}
